# shell: desktop
# home: fixture
# prefs: factory
# View > Highlight Caret Line and View > Block Caret, end to end.  A click
# puts the caret in the second line; with Highlight Caret Line the blank
# part of that row turns the theme's note colour while the first row
# stays white, and with Block Caret the character after the caret sits on
# a block of text ink.  Each pixel is asserted before the item is taken
# too, so what is proven is the change.
wait 25
click 980 30
wait 4
menu-pick Documents
wait 4
menu-pick Editor
wait 90
expect-title Edit
click 60 8
wait 6
menu-pick Open…
wait 40
expect-title Open
click 400 200
wait 6
click 675 413
wait 60
expect-no-title Open
click-window Edit
wait 25
click 282 172 1
wait 10
shot /tmp/tiku-editor-looks-off.png
note the second row's blank and the character after the caret, plain
expect-pixel 400 172 ffffff
expect-pixel 286 162 ffffff
click 212 8
wait 6
expect-menu Highlight Caret Line
menu-pick Highlight Caret Line
wait 25
shot /tmp/tiku-editor-looks-line.png
note the caret's row is filled and the first row is not
expect-pixel 400 172 ffffc8
expect-pixel 400 153 ffffff
expect-pixel 286 162 ffffc8
click 212 8
wait 6
expect-menu Block Caret
menu-pick Block Caret
wait 25
shot /tmp/tiku-editor-looks-block.png
note the character after the caret stands on a block
expect-pixel 286 162 000000
click 212 8
wait 6
menu-pick Highlight Caret Line
wait 6
click 212 8
wait 6
menu-pick Block Caret
wait 25
shot /tmp/tiku-editor-looks-again.png
note both taken off, the row and the character are plain again
expect-pixel 400 172 ffffff
expect-pixel 286 162 ffffff
note put the editor away rather than leave it standing
click-window Edit
wait 6
key Escape
wait 30
expect-no-title Edit
quit
